Keira Kernel
============

Keira is an educational, exploratory freestanding monolithic kernel in
Ring 0 built from scratch in safe Rust, Assembly and C, supporting both modern
64-bit (x86_64) and pure 32-bit (i686) bare-metal architectures.

Built as an open systems programming learning journey, Keira manages hardware
resources from first principles, coordinates modular device drivers and
provides a preemptive multitasking scheduler, multi-core Symmetric
Multiprocessing (SMP) with 16-bit real-mode AP bootstrap trampolines, 109 POSIX
system call numbers (89 active vectors), bare-metal TCP/IP stack, FAT12/16/32 and EXT4 filesystems, USTAR
initrd, canonical userspace init (PID 1, init.elf), standalone userspace shell (sh.elf)
with I/O redirection and pipelines, 46 native core utilities (cat.elf, chmod.elf, chown.elf, ls.elf, fetch.elf, ps.elf, kill.elf, hostname.elf, clear.elf, dmesg.elf, df.elf, ln.elf, mount.elf, umount.elf, test_pipe.elf, test_poll.elf, test_select.elf, pmap.elf, reboot.elf, poweroff.elf, grep.elf, wc.elf, head.elf, tail.elf, echo.elf, sleep.elf, etc.),
canonical shell script execution (shebang #!), direct $PATH execution, Ring 3 userland isolation with 41 verified master ABI security test suites, bare-metal io_uring asynchronous I/O engine, High-Precision
Event Timer (HPET) monotonic clocks, an interactive supervisor diagnostic shell with 32 commands and freestanding native C compiler (kcc.elf).

The Story & Learning Journey
----------------------------

Keira serves as a hands-on laboratory for understanding the core foundations
of computer systems across 56 chronological engineering milestones:

* Milestones 01-07: Bootstrap, Virtual Paging, Scheduler, Storage, Networking, Userland and Security.
* Milestones 08-15: Network Engine, Dual-Arch Parity, EBR, Work-Stealing, Slab and Multi-Threading.
* Milestones 16-24: Canonical FHS, Page Cache, Loopback, Pure Kernel, Shell and Complete Coreutils.
* Milestones 25-30: Network Diagnostics, Routing FIB, Netstat, Iptables and Multi-User Security.
* Milestones 31-36: Distro Purge, VFS Symlinks, Modular Decoupling, Pure Kernel Demarcation, Dynamic VFS Mounts and Dynamic Anonymous Pipes.
* Milestone 37: Text Processing Pipeline Suite (grep, wc, head, tail, echo, sleep), Kernel Stack Hardening and VMM Table Zeroing.
* Milestone 38: POSIX Discretionary Access Control (DAC), File Permissions (chmod), Ownership (chown) and Real Stat Metadata.
* Milestone 39: VFS Dentry Cache (dcache), Century Syscall 100 (fstat), Telemetry and Ring 0 Inspection.
* Milestone 40: Pure Kernel Memory Introspection (/proc/slabinfo, /proc/vmstat), Slab Cache Registry and Supervisor Telemetry.
* Milestone 41: VFS Inode Cache (icache), Cache Trinity Completion (page_cache, dcache, icache) and Ring 0 Inspection.
* Milestone 42: Pure Kernel Dynamic ProcFS Process Hierarchy (/proc/[pid]/stat, /proc/[pid]/statm), Dynamic PID Enumeration and Ring 0 pidinfo Command.
* Milestone 43: Hardware W^X & Kernel Exploit Mitigations (NX/DEP Bit 63, CR0.WP, CR4.SMEP/SMAP/UMIP Detection and Ring 0 secinfo Command).
* Milestone 44: Kernel ASLR (KASLR) & Userland Randomization (CPU RDRAND/RDSEED, SplitMix64 Mixer, Stack/Mmap/Brk ASLR and Ring 0 aslrinfo Command).
* Milestone 45: Dynamic Stack Canary & Cryptographic Hardware Entropy (AT_RANDOM Auxv, __stack_chk_guard, Century Syscall 101 SYS_GETRANDOM, /dev/urandom Silicon Integration and Ring 0 rnginfo Command).
* Milestone 46: Process Resource Limits (RLIMIT), Memory/FD Quota Enforcement & Process Sandboxing (SYS_GETRLIMIT, SYS_SETRLIMIT, SYS_PRLIMIT64, /proc/[pid]/limits and Ring 0 rlimit Command).
* Milestone 47: In-Kernel UNIX Domain Sockets (AF_UNIX), Zero-Copy Streaming, Century-Plus Syscall 105 (SYS_SOCKETPAIR), /proc/net/unix and Ring 0 unixsock Command.
* Milestone 48: Industrial-Grade Kernel Logging & Telemetry (32 KiB Ring Buffer, pr_* Macros, Syscall 44, Live dmesg).
* Milestone 49: CoW Fork Engine & Virtual Memory Fault Paging (O(1) Hash Table, Knuth Shift Deletion, In-Place Promotion, Ring 0 memory -c).
* Milestone 50: Virtual Memory Areas (VMA) Address Space Inspection & pmap Utility (VMA Table, /proc/[pid]/maps, Canonical Formatting, /bin/pmap, Ring 0 vmainfo).
* Milestone 51: Pure Kernel Demarcation, Application-Layer Bloat Purge & Canonical Rootfs Hardening (Ring 0 HTTPS Client Purge, Rootfs FHS Hardening, Freestanding Monolithic Kernel Purity).
* Milestone 52: POSIX Synchronous I/O Multiplexing & SYS_POLL (Century-Plus Syscall 106, /bin/test_poll, ABI Test 38, Ring 0 pollinfo).
* Milestone 53: POSIX Synchronous I/O Multiplexing Triad & SYS_SELECT (Century-Plus Syscall 107, /bin/test_select, ABI Test 39, Ring 0 selectinfo).
* Milestone 54: Pure Freestanding Monolithic Kernel Demarcation, Layer 7 HTTP Ring 0 Purge, BSD Socket Networking Engine & Canonical FHS Purification (Pure L4 TCP Transport, <arpa/inet.h>, /usr/src FHS Standardization).
* Milestone 55: POSIX System Identity Subsystem & SYS_UNAME (Century-Plus Syscall 108, struct utsname, <sys/utsname.h>, ABI Test 40, Pure uname).
* Milestone 56: POSIX System Power Management Subsystem & SYS_REBOOT (Century-Plus Syscall 109, <sys/reboot.h>, /bin/reboot, /bin/poweroff, ABI Test 41).

Read the detailed engineering journal: docs/journey/index.rst
System architecture overview: docs/arch/index.rst

Quick Start
-----------

* Get the latest code: https://github.com/mrvlous/keira
* Setup the workspace: See docs/process/build-system.rst
* Build & run 64-bit kernel: `make run` (or `make run-64`)
* Build & run 32-bit kernel: `make ARCH=i686 run` (or `make run-32`)
* Build & run with multi-core SMP: `make run SMP=4`
* Build pure Ring 0 kernel (no userland disk): `make run-bare`
* Build all architectures: `make full`
* Automated multi-architecture test suite: `make test-all`
* Build Sphinx technical documentation: `make docs`

